                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the multi-actor journey`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Experience on GharSe is <strong>co-created</strong> among consumers, professionals and your matching infrastructure — so diagnose where value is leaking AND for whom. Your verbatims are blunt: pros wait <em>seven days</em> for payouts and resolution is slow, while UrbanReady dangles ₹45,000 guarantees and a slicker flow. The trap is to read this as a consumer-funnel problem, or to chase the competitor's tactics, when the leak that is uniquely yours sits on the supply side. Build a two-sided pain map, find the co-created leaks, and name the single highest-leverage touchpoint.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Prioritize the touchpoint fix`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Invest the <strong>₹9 crore and 6 sprints</strong> across the fix menu. Every fix competes for the same finite pool, so a costed portfolio must fit both caps. At least one fix must protect the <strong>professional</strong> experience — a delightful consumer flow on a collapsing supply base is a slow-motion crisis. And on a two-sided platform you should keep a reserve: don't commit the whole budget before you've seen how supply responds, because the board will want a rebalancing move next month.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Tune matching & engineer trust`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Two intertwined platform levers. Your <strong>matching objective</strong> is not neutral: speed lifts the headline match rate but routes the hard jobs to your best pros and drops their earnings — <em>a 90% match that books the wrong pro is churn with a delay</em>. Your <strong>trust mechanism</strong> should absorb the specific risk that broke trust this quarter — the safety complaint — not stack every mechanism into trust theater that adds friction and suppresses conversion. Decide who wins, who loses, and how the two choices interact.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & rebalance the network`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The quarter's results land mixed — some consumer metrics up, supply churn still elevated, or vice versa. Pick <strong>one rebalancing move</strong> within your remaining budget to restore two-sided health. Handle the two viral incidents: a fast, visible <em>redo-or-refund</em> can turn detractors into promoters, while silence converts them into permanent critics. Then defend a genuine two-sided trajectory to the board — not a vanity match-rate headline that hides a supply crisis.`,

