                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_twosided:      `<strong>Good instinct.</strong> A two-sided pain map is the right lens — experience here is co-created, so a leak that only shows on the consumer dashboard may be rooted on the supply side. Now name the single highest-leverage touchpoint.`,
                    coach_r1_consumer:      `<strong>Careful — this is the consumer-only trap.</strong> You can polish the booking flow and lift NPS for a quarter, but if your best pros keep leaving at 8% a month, match quality craters and the consumers you delighted leave too. On a marketplace, who is your second customer?`,
                    coach_r1_competitor:    `<strong>You're fighting UrbanReady's battle on their terms.</strong> They offer guarantees, so you benchmark guarantees — but your verbatims say payouts take seven days and resolution is slow. What is the leak that is uniquely yours, and would fixing it retain pros more cheaply than a price war you can't win?`,
                    coach_r1_individuals:   `<strong>Blaming individuals is the comfortable answer.</strong> When top-pro churn doubles across the whole base, that's a system and experience problem, not careless people. Diagnose the payout delay, the matching, and the resolution flow — not the actors.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it two-sided AND located the leak in the slow T+7 payouts and weak resolution — the problem that is genuinely yours, not UrbanReady's. Fix that and you retain supply far more cheaply than matching their guarantees.`,
                    coach_r1_pricewar:      `<strong>Matching a ₹45,000 guarantee for everyone is a price war you can't win.</strong> It bleeds your take rate and still doesn't fix the seven-day payout wait that pros actually complain about. Diagnose your own leak before you reach for the competitor's chequebook.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's how the slide becomes a crisis.</strong> NPS down 15, match down 9, top-pro churn doubled and a competitor in three of your cities is not a bad quarter to ride out. There is a structural leak — find it.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You're diagnosing on gut feel.</strong> The funnels, NPS verbatims and matching dashboard are right there. Ground the diagnosis in the data or you'll spend ₹9 crore on the wrong touchpoint.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A genuinely two-sided portfolio.</strong> Faster payouts directly fix the supply leak, you've protected both sides, and you kept a reserve for the rebalancing move the board will demand next month. That is portfolio sequencing under a network constraint.`,
                    coach_r2_consumeronly:  `<strong>This portfolio is all consumer.</strong> A slicker booking flow and a redo guarantee lift NPS, but nothing here stops your best pros leaving for UrbanReady. Fund at least one supply fix — faster payouts is the cheapest, highest-leverage one you have.`,
                    coach_r2_noreserve:     `<strong>You've committed almost the whole budget.</strong> The plan is good, but on a two-sided platform supply rarely fixes itself on the first try — and you'll have nothing left when the board wants a pro-retention move next month. Why spend your entire reserve before you've seen how the other side responds?`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>A workable plan.</strong> You're inside both caps. Make sure the supply side is genuinely addressed and that you've kept room to rebalance once the results land.`,
                    coach_r3_fairness:      `<strong>Supply-fairness matching protects the network.</strong> Distributing jobs retains your mid-tier pros and stops your best ones absorbing every hard, low-margin job — slightly lower headline speed, far healthier supply and liquidity that lasts.`,
                    coach_r3_quality:       `<strong>Quality matching lifts consumer satisfaction</strong> but a longer wait can soften the headline match rate. It's defensible — just watch that you're not overloading your top pros with every hard job.`,
                    coach_r3_speed:         `<strong>Speed jumped your match rate — and that's the trap.</strong> Fastest-available routes the hard, low-margin jobs to your best pros, their effective earnings drop, and a 90% match that books the wrong pro is churn with a delay. What did speed cost you on the supply side?`,
                    coach_r3_price:         `<strong>Price-sensitive matching squeezes the pros twice</strong> — already-thin margins on hard jobs, now routed to the cheapest bidder. It dents both quality and supply health. This is not where your leak is.`,
                    coach_r3_targeted:      `<strong>Targeted trust architecture.</strong> The verification badge addresses the safety complaint that actually broke trust this quarter, paired with fairness matching that needs fewer trust interventions. One risk, one mechanism — not trust theater.`,
                    coach_r3_theater:       `<strong>That's trust theater.</strong> Badge, ratings, escrow AND insurance is four extra steps in the booking flow — trust friction now suppressing the very conversion you're trying to lift. Which single risk most damaged trust, and which one mechanism actually addresses it?`,
                    coach_r3_lowpay:        `<strong>You've left top-pro earnings exposed.</strong> With UrbanReady guaranteeing ₹45,000, doing little to protect effective pay keeps the churn engine running no matter how good the matching is. Defend the earnings of the pros who absorb your hardest jobs.`,
                    coach_r4_proearnings_right: `<strong>Right rebalance, right side.</strong> Your supply was the weak side, and a pro-earnings floor restores it directly — that's reading the mixed results and fixing the cross-side imbalance rather than feeding the side that's already winning.`,
                    coach_r4_proearnings:   `<strong>A pro-earnings floor is sound</strong> — protecting supply is rarely wrong on a marketplace. Just confirm it's the weakest side; if consumers are the ones still slipping, you may be reinforcing strength.`,
                    coach_r4_wrongside:     `<strong>You're feeding the side that's already winning.</strong> Consumer metrics are up but supply is your weak side — a consumer-trust booster widens the imbalance the network punishes. Rebalance toward the professionals before match quality follows them out the door.`,
                    coach_r4_consumertrust: `<strong>A consumer-trust booster helps</strong> — sensible if consumers are genuinely the weaker side. If supply is the one still bleeding, though, this is the wrong lever.`,
                    coach_r4_pricewar:      `<strong>Matching the ₹45,000 guarantee across the board buys supply at a brutal cost.</strong> It dents your take rate and your consumer experience funding, and it still doesn't fix the experience leaks that pros actually named. A price war is the move you reach for when you skipped the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>Nothing left to rebalance with.</strong> You committed the full budget earlier, so when supply didn't fix itself you had no move. That's exactly why a two-sided platform keeps a reserve.`,
                    coach_r4_recover_right: `<strong>Textbook recovery and rebalance.</strong> A fast, visible redo-or-refund turned the viral detractors into a recovery story, and the pro-earnings floor steadied the supply side — both sides healthier, the cross-side loop intact. That's the recovery paradox working for you.`,
                    coach_r4_quiet:         `<strong>Quiet, one-to-one recovery helps the individuals</strong> but the viral posts were public — handled invisibly, the wider audience still sees an unanswered complaint. A visible recovery does more for trust than a private one.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Hoping it blows over is the recovery error.</strong> Two viral posts and no response converts recoverable detractors into permanent critics — and the silence reads as confirmation. A fast, fair, visible redo-or-refund is what turns a detractor into a promoter.`,
                    coach_r4_consumeronly:  `<strong>'The rest will follow' rarely does on a marketplace.</strong> A pitch built only on NPS and repeat-usage hides whether supply can sustain it. The board's real question is whether this trajectory survives — answer it with both sides.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>A 90% match headline is exactly the vanity metric the board should distrust.</strong> If it was bought with speed matching that drove your pros out, the number collapses the moment supply does. Don't declare victory on a metric that hides a supply crisis.`,

